  "aiOverview": {
    "content": "Ratified in 1967, the 25th Amendment clarifies presidential succession and inability, establishing procedures for transferring power if a President dies, resigns, or becomes incapacitated. It ensures an orderly transfer of power, notably allowing the Vice President and Cabinet to declare a President unfit, making the VP Acting President, and providing a mechanism to fill vice presidential vacancies. Key aspects and provisions of the 25th Amendment include: Section 1 (Succession): Explicitly states that if the President is removed, dies, or resigns, the Vice President becomes President. Section 2 (Vacancy): Allows the President to nominate a Vice President, confirmed by a majority of both Houses of Congress, if a vacancy occurs. Section 3 (Voluntary Transfer): The President may declare in writing they are temporarily unable to discharge their duties (e.g., for surgery), making the VP Acting President until a written declaration to the contrary is submitted. Section 4 (Incapacity/Removal): The Vice President and a majority of the Cabinet can declare the President incapacitated, immediately elevating the VP to Acting President. If the President disputes this, Congress decides the issue, requiring a two-thirds vote in both houses to maintain the Vice President as Acting President. Purpose: The Amendment was created following the assassination of John F. Kennedy to address gaps in the Constitution regarding presidential disability and, specifically, to provide a clear procedure to remove a President unfit for office. History/Usage: The removal clause (Section 4) has never been invoked. However, it has been used for temporary transfers, such as when President Ronald Reagan and President George W. Bush underwent medical procedures. Benefits:The 25th Amendment removes uncertainty about who leads the country during a crisis, ensuring continuity of government and preventing a power vacuum, as shown in the Bipartisan Policy Center article and discussed on the History.com website. Cases/Usage: 1973: Vice President Gerald Ford was confirmed under Section 2 following Spiro Agnew's resignation. 1974: Gerald Ford assumed the presidency under Section 1 following Richard Nixon's resignation. 1985: President Ronald Reagan briefly transferred power to Vice President George H.W. Bush during surgery. 2002 & 2007: President George W. Bush transferred power to Vice President Dick Cheney during medical procedures.",
